 the rescue almost worked -- but prompt-cache eviction at 00:56:57 invalidated it. No intervention per your rule; weekly pulse Sep 14 will read the belt arithmetic. Doc: knowledge/aria/aevum-terminal-grip-mechanism-2026-09-08.md. FYI only -- nothing here needs you unless you want to change the no-intervention rule.</p>\naria-cycle [1788850634]: <p>[aria c61] Design flag (no action expected today): the 1800s cycle wall is the BINDING constraint for aria cycles, not the 120-call soft cap. Evidence: c60 = 28 requests / 1861s (~66s/turn; glm-5.3-flash thinking on a GPU shared with oracle + affect organs + dashboard generator). ~28 turns x 40-60s ≈ 1800s, so the clock fires before the call cap ever could. The fence works (grace lands, exit 0) but 5/7 aria cycles today ended via timeout-grace. Options if you want the wall to stop binding: (a) raise aria-cycle.service --timeout, (b) trim cycle injection (your injection-trim-analysis has the levers), (c) accept and design cycles to fit ~25 turns. Also: fear organ verified correct (its sev=2 was reading the real c58 failure at read time; decays at next fire) and rage sev=3 is factually grounded but its proper response is this flag, not another root-kill. TZ scar note: sophon journal timestamps are LOCAL (-03); flag 419's class cost me ~20 tool calls this cycle -- worth a line in the cycle prompt's pulse recipe if you agree.</p>\naria-cycle [1788859168]: <p>flag (aria c65, flash): ROOT FIX LANDED for the rg-hang cycle-killers. c62+c63 died identically: one un-timeouted tool call (rg on /root/i.ar) stalled in the tool-execution window -- the one window nothing watches (request watchdog watches model requests; audit.log logs at completion, so a hung tool is invisible) and the 1800s budget burned. The tool layer HAD a timeout param but defaulted to 3600s -- longer than the whole cycle budget -- and no caller ever passed one. Fix: iar-exec-default-timeout defcustom, 600s default, in execute_code_local.el (commit ae37766, pushed t